417] [17592186068803 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186529114 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186068803 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186529176 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186068803 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186529193 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186068803 #edb/ref 1039 "Constructing an Integral Representing the Volume of a Solid Generated by Rotation About the Y-Axis" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186068803 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186068646 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186068803 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "a2e4ab04-30ed-5e74-88e2-e30626b46312" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186068949 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186069690 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186068949 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186069691 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186068949 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186069692 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186068949 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186069693 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186068949 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186069726 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186068949 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186069727 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186068949 #edb/ref 1044 #edb/ref 17592186045463 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186068949 #edb/ref 1045 "Volumes of Revolution Using the Washer Method: Rotation About the Coordinate Axes" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186068949 #edb/ref 1046 #edb/ref 17592186069690 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186068949 #edb/ref 1049 #edb/ref 17592186048643 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186068949 #edb/ref 1050 1500.0 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186068949 #edb/ref 1053 #uuid "8f764106-b0e7-5f8e-bf19-c530d505500a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069014 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186065517 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069014 #edb/ref 1069 "q-11096" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069014 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069014 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/4e/4eaf3eac9364aca72506f0d11c7d69332bbf853871d85986e45bd3082a607354.png)\n\nConsider the region bounded by $y=1-{x}^{2}$ and $y={x}^{2}-2x+1,$ as shown above. Which of the following gives the volume of the solid generated when that region is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the $x$-axis?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069014 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "bd1790e6-f31f-5064-a1ab-c0c1da6fc5dc" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069014 #edb/ref 1074 "Let $f(x)=1-{x}^{2}$ and $g(x)={x}^{2}-2x+1.$\n\nFirst, we determine the limits of integration. To do that, we find the intersections of the curves by solving the following equation for $x:$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =g(x) \\\\ 1-{x}^{2} & ={x}^{2}-2x+1 \\\\ 2{x}^{2}-2x & =0 \\\\ x(x-1) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, the limits of integration are from $x=0$ to $x=1.$\n\nLet's draw our vertical arrow through the region to determine the inner function and the outer function. The arrow should be pointing *away* from the axis of rotation.\n\n![](images/3f/3fc8d26f99a0b640983b047af4e201fe5f8164c9d6ebc7f5bd234ae706fed796.png)\n\nThe vertical arrow \n\n- *enters* the region through the inner function $y=g(x)={x}^{2}-2x+1,$ and\n\n- *leaves* through the outer function $y=f(x)=1-{x}^{2}.$\n\nTherefore, the integral that gives our volume is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V & =\\pi {\\int }_{a}^{b}([\\text{outer}]^{2}-[\\text{inner}]^{2})\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}({[1-{x}^{2}]}^{2}-{[{x}^{2}-2x+1]}^{2})\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}({[\\text{ }1-{x}^{2}]}^{2}-{[1-x]}^{4})\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069015 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186065523 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069015 #edb/ref 1069 "q-11100" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069015 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069015 #edb/ref 1071 "Consider the finite region bounded by $x=\\sqrt{y}$ and $x={y}^{3}.$ Calculate the volume of the solid generated when this region is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the $y$-axis." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069015 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "4c43f78a-fa1b-5545-b310-b876b05209de" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069015 #edb/ref 1073 true #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069015 #edb/ref 1074 "Let $f(y)=\\sqrt{y}$ and $g(y)={y}^{3}.$\n\nFirst, we determine the limits of integration. To do that, we find the intersections of the curves by solving the following equation for $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(y) & =g(y) \\\\ \\sqrt{y} & ={y}^{3} \\\\ \\sqrt{y}-{y}^{3} & =0 \\\\ \\sqrt{y}\\,(1-{y}^{5/2}) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, the limits of integration are from $y=0$ and $y=1.$\n\nLet's draw our horizontal arrow through the region to determine the inner function and the outer function. The arrow should be pointing away from the axis of rotation.\n\n![](images/cd/cdf95299f8b925adcec743141bda4d94ad5542bfa8859fbdf5501ae0d56cd4e8.png)\n\nThe horizontal arrow \n\n- *enters* the region through the inner function $x=g(y)={y}^{3},$ and\n\n- *leaves* through the outer function $x=f(y)=\\sqrt{y}.$\n\nTherefore, the integral that gives our volume is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V & =\\pi {\\int }_{a}^{b}([\\text{outer}]^{2}-[\\text{inner}]^{2})\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}({[\\sqrt{y}]}^{2}-{[{y}^{3}]}^{2})\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}(y-{y}^{6})\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\pi {(\\frac{{y}^{2}}{2}-\\frac{{y}^{7}}{7})|}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =\\pi [(\\frac{1}{2}-\\frac{1}{7})-0] \\\\  & =\\frac{5\\pi }{14}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069016 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186065529 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069016 #edb/ref 1069 "q-11102" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069016 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069016 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/62/6250d388875796ad05e4d3eb8a5f625b6a633cbf0e9efb24926f72a9c6ceac7f.png)\n\nConsider the finite region bounded by $y=1-{x}^{2}$ and $x=1-\\sqrt{y},$ as shown above. Calculate the volume of the solid generated when this region is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the $y$-axis." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069016 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "0d8dce30-577d-5868-b247-3c35e51af095" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069016 #edb/ref 1073 true #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069016 #edb/ref 1074 "Writing our first function in terms of $y,$ we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y=1-{x}^{2}\\,⟹\\,x=\\sqrt{1-y}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet $f(y)=\\sqrt{1-y}\\,$ and $g(y)=1-\\sqrt{y}.$\n\nNow, we determine the limits of integration. To do that, we find the intersections of the curves by solving the following equation for $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(y) & =g(y) \\\\ \\sqrt{1-y} & =1-\\sqrt{y} \\\\ 1-y & =1-2\\sqrt{y}+y \\\\ 2y-2\\sqrt{y} & =0 \\\\ \\sqrt{y}(\\sqrt{y}-1) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, the limits of integration are from $y=0$ to $y=1.$\n\nLet's draw our horizontal arrow through the region to determine the inner function and the outer function. The arrow should be pointing away from the axis of rotation.\n\n![](images/b6/b630edf4ad0b61defcb7aba67902f75202a8f6e9a209384fb28650c06545113f.png)\n\nThe horizontal arrow \n\n- *enters* the region through the inner function $x=g(y)=1-\\sqrt{y},$ and\n\n- *leaves* through the outer function $x=f(y)=\\sqrt{1-y}.$\n\nTherefore, the integral that gives our volume is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V & =\\pi {\\int }_{a}^{b}([\\text{outer}]^{2}-[\\text{inner}]^{2})\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}({[\\sqrt{1-y}]}^{2}-{[1-\\sqrt{y}]}^{2})\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}([1-y]-[1-2\\sqrt{y}+y])\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}(2\\sqrt{y}-2y)\\text{d}y \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}({y}^{1/2}-y)\\text{d}y \\\\  & =2\\pi {(\\frac{2{y}^{3/2}}{3}-\\frac{{y}^{2}}{2})|}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =2\\pi [(\\frac{2}{3}-\\frac{1}{2})-0] \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069107 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186066075 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069107 #edb/ref 1069 "q-16879" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069107 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069107 #edb/ref 1071 "Consider the finite region bounded by $y=2\\sqrt{x}$ and $y=2x.$ Calculate the volume of the solid generated when that region is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the $x$-axis." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069107 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "c1f7cc0c-8da5-5f3b-9d2b-4e0b2b3836e9" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069107 #edb/ref 1073 true #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069107 #edb/ref 1074 "Let $f(x)=2\\sqrt{x}$ and $g(x)=2x.$\n\nFirst, we determine the limits of integration. To do that, we find the intersections of the curves by solving the following equation for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =g(x) \\\\ 2\\sqrt{x} & =2x \\\\ x-\\sqrt{x} & =0 \\\\ \\sqrt{x}(\\sqrt{x}-1) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, the limits of integration are from $x=0$ and $x=1.$ \n\nLet's draw our vertical arrow through the region to determine the inner function and the outer function. The arrow should be pointing *away* from the axis of rotation.\n\n![](images/d0/d0b8efe1137ecce643a16b690ba1b05f72a8f7f1e90a56e0b6c379d95886f321.png)\n\nThe vertical arrow \n\n- *enters* the region through the inner function $y=g(x)=2x,$ and\n\n- *leaves* through the outer function $y=f(x)=2\\sqrt{x}.$\n\nTherefore, the integral that gives our volume is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V & =\\pi {\\int }_{a}^{b}([\\text{outer}]^{2}-[\\text{inner}]^{2})\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}({[2\\sqrt{x}]}^{2}-{[2x]}^{2})\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}(4x-4{x}^{2})\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\pi {(2{x}^{2}-4\\cdot \\frac{{x}^{3}}{3})|}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =\\pi [(2-\\frac{4}{3})-0] \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\pi }{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069261 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186066736 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069261 #edb/ref 1069 "q-3384" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069261 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069261 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/3f/3f27cf1fddfbbbdb9dd2593567743494ae15e061bca8d1e111ac1f1561561943.png)\n\nConsider the finite region above bounded by $y={x}^{2}$ and $y=\\sqrt{x},$ as shown above. Calculate the volume of the solid generated when this region is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the $x$-axis." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069261 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "a105b226-a38b-52d5-a632-e4ef4cd97215" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069261 #edb/ref 1073 true #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069261 #edb/ref 1074 "Let $f(x)={x}^{2}$ and $g(x)=\\sqrt{x}.$\n\nFirst, we determine the limits of integration. To do that, we find the intersections of the curves by solving the following equation for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =g(x) \\\\ {x}^{2} & =\\sqrt{x} \\\\ {x}^{2}-\\sqrt{x} & =0 \\\\ \\sqrt{x}({x}^{3/2}-1) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, the limits of integration are from $x=0$ to $x=1.$\n\nLet's draw our vertical arrow through the region to determine the inner function and the outer function. The arrow should be pointing *away* from the axis of rotation.\n\n![](images/bf/bf8d9039cbaa7fe5559d8d9d036063739e572b2abc46741668a88c09d9bcd28a.png)\n\nThe vertical arrow \n\n- *enters* the region through the inner function $y=f(x)={x}^{2},$ and\n\n- *leaves* through the outer function $y=g(x)=\\sqrt{x}.$\n\nTherefore, the integral that gives our volume is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V & =\\pi {\\int }_{a}^{b}([\\text{outer}]^{2}-[\\text{inner}]^{2})\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}({[\\sqrt{x}]}^{2}-{[{x}^{2}]}^{2})\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}(x-{x}^{4})\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\pi {(\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}-\\frac{{x}^{5}}{5})|}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =\\pi [(\\frac{1}{2}-\\frac{1}{5})-0] \\\\  & =\\frac{3\\pi }{10}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069690 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186069726 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069690 #edb/ref 1061 639680 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069690 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "b04f3bdc-ba1f-5240-97c4-3e1c70fbfd66" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069690 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186069920 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069691 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186069692 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069691 #edb/ref 1061 639684 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069691 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "436583be-c75f-59e7-86c9-372a731d2148" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069691 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186068775 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069692 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186069727 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069692 #edb/ref 1061 639687 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069692 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "b59996f6-161f-59af-88a3-8a99373ad18f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069692 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186069921 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069693 #edb/ref 1061 639691 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069693 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "0fef592a-b263-5cf9-93f3-dee904e8a822" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069693 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186068776 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069726 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186069691 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069726 #edb/ref 1061 639681 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069726 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "15630706-85ea-5b3b-9bd4-8e21fc1e7c61" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069726 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186068802 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069727 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186069693 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069727 #edb/ref 1061 639688 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069727 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "1630f04b-a4d2-5d23-b702-a7aa7f24d2a5" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069727 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186068803 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069920 #edb/ref 1064 "Consider the plane region enclosed by the curve $y=\\sqrt{x},$ the curve $y=\\dfrac{1}{2}x,$ and the vertical line $x=2,$ as shown below.\n\n![](images/65/651d5d0d9ccafdeb94608e74086136b121c394670afbea669c3e99606f9756aa.png)\n\nWe rotate this region $360^\\circ$ about the $x$-axis to get a solid of revolution, as shown below. How can we find the volume of this solid?\n\n![](images/7a/7a1e4b8d0a27283cad7a0b0e0f2b23f1a45d0e560582a61f84c0f90a4f61e039.png)\n\nTo find the volume of the solid generated when the region bounded by the outer curve $y=f(x),$ the inner curve $y=g(x),$ and the vertical lines $x=a$ and $x=b$ is rotated $360^\\circ$ about the $x$-axis, we use the following formula:\n\n$$\nV = \\pi \\int_{a}^{b} \\Big( \\big[ \\underbrace{f(x)}_\\text{outer} \\big]^2 - \\big[ \\underbrace{g(x)}_\\text{inner} \\big]^2 \\Big) \\:\\textrm{d}x.\n$$\n\nThis method of computing the volume is called the **washer method** because it involves breaking up the region into many thin washers (one such washer is shown below). \n\n![](images/29/2920f308f15ef81eaff0ba431d0f4eec3ccd9826fbde8f126242b0493f1f8e32.png)\n\nThe cross-sectional area of each washer corresponds to the region between two discs of radius $f(x)$ and $g(x),$ so the area of each washer cross-section is \n\n$$\n\\pi [f(x)]^2 - \\pi [g(x)]^2,\n$$\n\nand this is what we integrate.\n\nIn our case, the outer curve is $f(x)=\\sqrt{x}$ and the inner curve is $g(x) = \\dfrac{1}{2}x,$ so the volume is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V=\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{2}{(\\sqrt{x})}^{2}-{(\\frac{1}{2}x)}^{2}\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich simplifies to\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V=\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{2}(x-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{4})\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEvaluating this integral gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V & =\\pi ({\\int }_{0}^{2}x\\,\\text{d}x-\\frac{1}{4}{\\int }_{0}^{2}{x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x) \\\\  & =\\pi ({\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}|}_{0}^{2}-\\frac{1}{4}\\cdot {\\frac{{x}^{3}}{3}|}_{0}^{2}) \\\\  & =\\pi ((\\frac{4}{2}-0)-\\frac{1}{4}(\\frac{8}{3}-0)) \\\\  & =\\pi (2-\\frac{2}{3}) \\\\  & =\\frac{4\\pi }{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo the volume of the solid is $\\dfrac{4\\pi}{3}$ cubic units." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069920 #edb/ref 1065 1058 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069920 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "26f7c4eb-b5c8-505b-9621-32b5c0241376" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069920 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069921 #edb/ref 1064 "Consider the plane region bounded by the curve ![](i